WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.200
Today is going to be all&nbsp;
about simulating evolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:17.120
on our computers. This can build virtual cars,&nbsp;
virtual creatures, even paint the Mona Lisa!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.920
These all sound amazing, but,&nbsp;
evolution on a computer? How?

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:31.120
A few years ago, a really fun online app surfaced&nbsp;
that used a genetic algorithm to evolve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:38.720
morphology of a simple 2D car with the goal of&nbsp;
having it roll as far away from a starting point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:46.000
as possible. A genetic algorithm? What is&nbsp;
that? Well, it is a super simple technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.120
where we start out from a set of random solutions,&nbsp;
and likely find out that none of them really work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:01.360
well. However, we start combining and mutating,&nbsp;
or in other words, changing small parts of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:11.040
solution, until, oh yes! Something starts to&nbsp;
move. Now as soon at least one wheel is placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.480
correctly, the algorithm will recognize&nbsp;
that this one rolled so much further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:23.360
and keep the genes of this solution in the&nbsp;
pool to breed new, similar solutions from it.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.760
A similar concept can also be used to design&nbsp;
the optimal morphology of a virtual creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:36.560
to make it skip forward faster. And,&nbsp;
look! That is so cool - over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.120
the technique learns to more efficiently&nbsp;
navigate a flat terrain by redesigning its legs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.280
that are now reminiscent of small springs&nbsp;
and uses them to skip its way forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.560
And here comes something even cooler - if we&nbsp;
change the terrain, the design of an effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:03.520
agent also changes accordingly, and the super&nbsp;
interesting part here is that it came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:12.400
an asymmetric design that is able to climb&nbsp;
stairs and travel uphill efficiently. Loving it!

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:19.760
And, in this new paper, scientists at Google Brain&nbsp;
tried to turbocharge an evolutionary algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.000
to be able to take a bunch of transparent&nbsp;
triangles, and get it to reorganize them so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.520
will paint the Mona Lisa for us. Or any image in&nbsp;
particular. The technique they are using is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.040
evolution strategies, and they say that it is&nbsp;
much faster at this than previous techniques.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:46.080
Well, I will believe it when I see it. Hold&nbsp;
on to your papers, and let’s see together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:54.480
Here is a basic evolutionary algorithm after 10&nbsp;
thousand steps. Well, it is getting there, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:01.040
it’s not that great. And, let’s see how well their&nbsp;
new method does with the same amount of steps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:10.320
Wow. My goodness! That is so much better. In fact,&nbsp;
their 10 thousand steps is close to equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.640
to half a million steps with a previous algorithm.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:22.080
And we are not done yet. Not even close. This&nbsp;
paper has two more amazing surprises. Surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:29.280
number one. We can even combine it with OpenAI’s&nbsp;
technique called CLIP, this learns about pairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.000
of images and their captions describing these&nbsp;
images, which is an important part of DALL-E 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.320
their amazing image generator AI. This&nbsp;
could take completely outlandish concepts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.440
and create beautiful, photorealistic images out&nbsp;
of it that are often as good as we would expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:56.720
from a good human artist. Scholars holding&nbsp;
on to their papers, a cyberfrog, you name it.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:03.760
So, get this, similarly to that, we can now&nbsp;
even write a piece of text, and the evolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.680
triangle builder will try to create an image&nbsp;
that fits this text description. With that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:17.200
we can request a triangle version of a&nbsp;
self-portrait, a human, and look at how similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:25.760
they are. Food for thought! But it can also try to&nbsp;
draw Walt Disney World, that is remarkable. Look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:34.080
at how beautifully it boils it down to its essence&nbsp;
with as few as 200, or even just 25 triangles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.160
Loving it. Also, drawing the Google&nbsp;
headquarters? No problem at all.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.920
And here comes surprise number&nbsp;
two. The authors claim that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:52.800
even faster than a differentiable renderer.&nbsp;
These are really powerful optimization techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:05:01.520
that can even grow this beautiful statue out of&nbsp;
nothing. And do all that in just a few steps. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.960
claiming that it is even faster than&nbsp;
that? Well, that is very ambitious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:15.840
Let’s have a look. And…oh yes, as expected the&nbsp;
differentiable technique creates a beautiful image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:24.560
very quickly. Now, try to beat that! Wow…the new&nbsp;
method converges to the final image even quicker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:32.480
That speed is simply stunning. Interestingly, they&nbsp;
also have different styles. The differentiable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:39.120
renderer introduces textures that are not&nbsp;
present in the original image, while the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:46.640
technique uses large triangles to create a smooth&nbsp;
approximation of the background and the hair, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:54.560
uses smaller ones to get a better approximation&nbsp;
of the intricate details of the face. Loving it.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:01.520
Now, let’s ramp up the challenge, and test&nbsp;
these a little more and add some prompts. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:08.240
oh yes, this is why the differentiable&nbsp;
technique flounders on the prompts. Look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.000
It almost seems to try to do everything&nbsp;
all at once. While, the new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:19.840
starts out from a fair approximation, and&nbsp;
converges to a great solution super quickly.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.920
I hope that this new take on evolution&nbsp;
strategies will be plugged in to applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:32.080
where differentiable techniques do well,&nbsp;
and perhaps do even better. That would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:38.080
absolutely amazing because these are a treasure&nbsp;
trove of science-fiction like applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.640
For instance, we would be able to&nbsp;
almost instantly solve this billiard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.160
game, where we would like to hit the white&nbsp;
ball with just the right amount of force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.640
and from the right direction, such that the&nbsp;
blue ball ends up close to the black spot. Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.800
simulate ink with a checkerboard pattern, and&nbsp;
exert just the appropriate forces so that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:06.000
forms exactly the Yin-Yang symbol shortly after.
Or, here comes a previous, science-fiction like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.400
example. This previous differentiable technique&nbsp;
adds carefully crafted ripples to the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.880
to make sure that it ends up in a state that&nbsp;
distorts the image of the squirrel in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:27.280
a powerful and well-known neural network sees&nbsp;
it not as a squirrel, but as a goldfish. Wow.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.920
And, if this new evolutionary&nbsp;
technique could do all of these tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.520
but better and faster? Sign me up&nbsp;
right now. What a time to be alive!

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

